                <pb facs="0489" n="457" xml:id="pg457"/>

                <head>164. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in the United
                        Kingdom<anchor n="1" xml:id="d164-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1957-05-17T13:21:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 17,
                                1957—1:21 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>8070. Please deliver following from President to Macmillan: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Dear Harold: Foster has just been talking to me about the China trade
                            situation and the message which he is sending to Selwyn Lloyd<anchor
                                n="2" xml:id="d164-f2"/> in reply to SELWYN’s memorandum which he
                            gave to Jock Whitney on the evening of May 13th.<anchor n="3"
                                xml:id="d164-f3"/></p>
                        <p>Our military advisers are strongly of the opinion that many of the items
                            which you would take off the China list will in fact appreciably help
                            the Chinese Communists to build up the military potential which
                            threatens us in this area and which we have the primary responsibility
                            to resist. Our Congress, although less well informed on the technical
                            details, feels strongly on this issue. As Foster is saying to SELWYN we
                            do not feel confident that increasing the list will really help your
                            trade very much. May it not mean merely that China’s trade with you will
                            consist of the more strategic rather than the less strategic items?</p>
                        <p>We are, however, giving some new discretion to our representatives in
                            Paris and hope very much that you will try to meet us as it would be
                            unfortunate in many respects if we should split publicly on this
                            issue.</p>
                        <p>Faithfully yours, D.E.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Please observe Presidential Handling.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
